-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Blotchy Surfaces:** Without the identity regularization term $L_{ID}$, Gaussian primitives tend to adjust their colors instead of allowing the appearance model to optimize, causing blotchy homogeneous surfaces [S1].
-*   **Unpleasant Colors:** The regularization term $L_{ID}$ prevents unpleasant colors by constraining the transformation to be close to the identity transformation [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Querying the appearance model for every pixel is time-consuming. The cell-based query approach is used to minimize optimization time [S1].
-*   **Depth Accuracy:** The method uses mean depth calculated during differentiable rasterization for back-projection [S1].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The cell-based query reduces optimization time compared to per-pixel querying [S1]. The appearance module is discarded after optimization, so it does not impact rendering speed [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Minimal memory overhead due to the lightweight MLP and hash grids [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Plug-and-play, decouples global and local appearance variations, ensures 3D consistency, minimal training time and memory usage, does not compromise rendering speed [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires differentiable rasterization to render depth [S1]. Performance depends on the accuracy of the depth map for back-projection [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Scenes with complex appearance variations, including global changes (e.g., daylight, ISPs) and local changes (e.g., street lamps) [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Scenes with mild appearance variations where coupled methods might suffice, or where real-time rendering speed is critical and the appearance module cannot be discarded (though DAVIGS allows discarding it) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The cell-based query reduces optimization time by computing transformation matrices for cells rather than every pixel. However, querying hash grids and interpolating matrices adds overhead compared to standard 3DGS [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing multi-resolution hash grids and an MLP, in addition to standard Gaussian parameters [S1].
+*   **Rendering Speed:** Rendering speed is not compromised because the appearance module is discarded after optimization. The method transforms rendered results at the image level, making it plug-and-play [S1].
+*   **Strengths:**
+    *   Mitigates floaters and color distortions caused by appearance variations [S1].
+    *   Decouples global and local appearance variations [S1].
+    *   Ensures 3D consistency across views [S1].
+    *   Low optimization time and memory overhead compared to coupled methods [S1].
+*   **Limitations:**
+    *   Requires rendering a depth map alongside the color image [S1].
+    *   Performance on scenes with only mild global variations may not be significantly better than standard 3DGS, as it is designed for complex appearance variations [S1].
 
 ## Background
-DAVIGS (Decoupling Appearance Variations In GS) was proposed by Dahmani et al. in 2024 [S1]. It addresses the limitations of existing Gaussian Splatting methods that are either tightly coupled with the rendering process (hindering real-time rendering) or only account for mild global variations (performing poorly in scenes with local light changes) [S1]. The method builds on 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) and incorporates multi-resolution hash grids and a lightweight MLP to model appearance variations at the image level [S1].
+DAVIGS (Decoupling Appearance Variations In GS) was proposed to address the limitations of existing Gaussian Splatting appearance modeling methods, which are either tightly coupled with the rendering process (hindering real-time rendering) or only account for mild global variations (performing poorly in scenes with local light changes) [S1]. It builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting representation and rendering pipeline [G]. The method was validated on several appearance-variant scenes, demonstrating state-of-the-art rendering quality with minimal training time and memory usage [S1].
 
